1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It was once thought of as an impossible dream, an inconceivable concept.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Now it's the most feared jet of the new millennium.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
All but undetectable to radar, it is the ace card in modern aerial warfare,

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
designed to strike before anyone knows it's coming.

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:26,000
The top secret world of stealth, the F-117 Nighthawk, next on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,520
Washington, March 19, 2003.

7
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:46,660
At 7.12 Eastern Standard Time, President Bush issues an executive order.

8
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:49,640
Its duration is to apply decisive force.

9
00:00:51,580 --> 00:00:56,680
Minutes later, two F-117 Nighthawks take to the pre-dawn sky.

10
00:00:57,720 --> 00:00:59,600
Their destination, Baghdad.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Their target, a reinforced bunker believed to house Iraq's dictator and his henchmen.

12
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:19,720
Undetected, the black jets pierce the most heavily defended airspace on Earth and prepare

13
00:01:19,721 --> 00:01:27,220
for a decapitation strike, one that could end a war even before it begins.

14
00:01:27,221 --> 00:01:35,840
This is stealth warfare.

15
00:01:35,841 --> 00:01:40,340
For as long as aircraft have been used in combat, efforts to make them invisible to

16
00:01:40,341 --> 00:01:43,880
the enemy have been ongoing.

17
00:01:43,881 --> 00:01:48,900
By the First World War, attempts to build stealth aircraft concentrated on the use of

18
00:01:48,901 --> 00:01:54,780
camouflage to make planes difficult to detect visually.

19
00:01:54,781 --> 00:01:59,680
For the Second World War, the development of camouflage paint has reached new levels

20
00:01:59,681 --> 00:02:07,620
of sophistication, but these traditional schemes can only delay visual detection.

21
00:02:07,621 --> 00:02:12,400
The invention of radar changes everything.

22
00:02:12,401 --> 00:02:17,840
By transmitting electromagnetic waves and observing the returning echoes, aircraft can

23
00:02:17,841 --> 00:02:24,100
now be spotted hundreds of miles away.

24
00:02:24,101 --> 00:02:29,500
To counter this, British engineers come up with chaff, narrow metallic strips that act

25
00:02:29,501 --> 00:02:31,600
as tiny reflectors.

26
00:02:31,601 --> 00:02:38,800
In the ensuing whiteout, enemy radar is confused.

27
00:02:38,801 --> 00:02:41,960
But it is not an entirely satisfactory solution.

28
00:02:41,961 --> 00:02:45,980
The enemy knows something is there, just not exactly where.

29
00:02:45,981 --> 00:02:52,560
There were certainly efforts during World War II, you know, with dropping all these

30
00:02:52,561 --> 00:03:00,320
little metal streamers to try to prevent people from using radar to see your airplane.

31
00:03:00,321 --> 00:03:04,900
So stealth has been tried since that period.

32
00:03:04,901 --> 00:03:12,480
By the 1950s, the Soviets are developing a highly sophisticated and lethal defense

33
00:03:12,481 --> 00:03:19,500
network consisting of SAMs, radar-guided surface-to-air missiles.

34
00:03:19,501 --> 00:03:31,040
Once they lock onto a target, these missiles will not miss.

35
00:03:31,041 --> 00:03:39,920
The development of these weapons sends shockwaves around the world.

36
00:03:39,921 --> 00:03:44,420
To counter this threat, the U.S. begins to produce aircraft that can fly higher than

37
00:03:44,421 --> 00:03:47,620
the enemy's planes and missiles.

38
00:03:47,621 --> 00:03:50,440
Lockheed's U-2 is the first of this new breed.

39
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:58,180
The U-2 is an attempt to keep an airplane from being struck down by enemy fire.

40
00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:03,080
And we're looking at altitude being its primary defense against missile threats from the ground.

41
00:04:03,780 --> 00:04:06,960
But in May 1960, disaster strikes.

42
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:13,580
A U-2 flown by Francis Gary Powers over the Soviet Union is shot down by a Sam.

43
00:04:13,581 --> 00:04:19,500
flying at high altitude is no longer sufficient.

44
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:22,720
Lockheed adds speed to the equation.

45
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:29,200
Flying at Mach 3 at 80,000 feet, the SR-71 Blackbird is untouchable.

46
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:34,340
But the reconnaissance aircraft is still detectable to radar.

47
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:37,320
Although no Blackbird is ever shot down,

48
00:04:37,321 --> 00:04:40,740
the Soviets come very close on more than one occasion.

49
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:50,580
During the Vietnam War, anti-aircraft systems massively disrupt U.S. air operations.

50
00:04:50,581 --> 00:05:01,580
We had a tough time with their SA-2s and SA-3s, and we knew that they were developing much more sophisticated missiles.

51
00:05:01,581 --> 00:05:11,580
We knew that just conventional airplanes were not going to be able to survive in a high-dense, coordinated missile defense system.

52
00:05:11,581 --> 00:05:25,380
In 1973, during the Yom Kippur War, the Israeli Air Force loses 109 strike aircraft in just 18 days.

53
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:36,060
Extrapolating the Israeli loss ratio into a NATO-Warsaw pact scenario,

54
00:05:36,061 --> 00:05:41,820
US strategists realize that NATO air forces would be no match for the Soviet missiles.

55
00:05:41,821 --> 00:05:46,920
The air force were recognizing the fact that there was no practical way of penetrating

56
00:05:46,921 --> 00:05:52,180
the Soviet defenses with our existing air forces, period.

57
00:05:52,181 --> 00:05:57,660
And so there was a requirement for a radical change in the stealth capabilities of an airplane

58
00:05:57,661 --> 00:06:02,220
to penetrate.

59
00:06:02,221 --> 00:06:07,880
For the aircraft industry, stealth represents a radical change in design, an engineering

60
00:06:07,881 --> 00:06:10,920
step too big to even contemplate.

61
00:06:10,921 --> 00:06:19,240
Manufacturers, for some reason, didn't pay much attention to this in the 50s and 60s

62
00:06:19,241 --> 00:06:21,520
and even in the 70s.

63
00:06:21,521 --> 00:06:25,940
And I think one of the reasons for that is the amount of radar cross-section reduction

64
00:06:25,941 --> 00:06:31,200
you had to make to go from a conventional airplane to a truly stealthy airplane,

65
00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:37,980
the number was so big that most people dismissed it as something.

66
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:39,360
Why waste your time on that?

67
00:06:40,580 --> 00:06:46,720
But by early 1974, DARPA, the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency,

68
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:50,540
has become sufficiently worried about the Soviet-made SAMs.

69
00:06:50,541 --> 00:06:57,540
It asks five manufacturers to identify a means of making an aircraft undetectable to radar.

70
00:06:57,541 --> 00:07:04,540
Surprisingly, Lockheed, the makers of the U-2 and SR-71, is overlooked.

71
00:07:04,541 --> 00:07:16,540
In 1975, Clarence Kelly Johnson retires as president of Lockheed's Advanced Development Program, known as the Skunk Works.

72
00:07:16,541 --> 00:07:17,241
works.

73
00:07:17,242 --> 00:07:24,240
His successor, Ben Rich, hears about the competition and lobbies hard to get the

74
00:07:24,241 --> 00:07:27,917
skunkworks into the thick of the action.

75
00:07:27,918 --> 00:07:32,880
Dennis Overhauser is tapped to head up the development

76
00:07:32,881 --> 00:07:33,581
team.

77
00:07:33,582 --> 00:07:41,060
One day while conducting research, he comes across an obscure paper written in 1962

78
00:07:41,061 --> 00:07:43,000
by a Soviet scientist.

79
00:07:43,760 --> 00:07:45,860
It contains a mathematical formula

80
00:07:45,861 --> 00:07:49,180
for calculating the radar return of a flat object.

81
00:07:51,020 --> 00:07:53,680
The paper, ignored by the Soviets,

82
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,480
is the spark that ignites the Skunk Works team.

83
00:07:57,260 --> 00:07:59,940
We had a team of people who were not just competent,

84
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:01,960
they were very experienced.

85
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:03,400
And I think that's key.

86
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:05,800
In other words, these people had done the SR-71,

87
00:08:06,100 --> 00:08:07,000
they'd done the U-2,

88
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:09,180
and could pick up a pencil and say,

89
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:10,880
okay, you want a wing structure designed?

90
00:08:11,060 --> 00:08:12,980
Just show me what you want. I'll design it.

91
00:08:13,860 --> 00:08:18,940
The Skunk Works team now considers how to make the aircraft invisible to radar.

92
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:26,100
If I want to reduce the range at which I detect an airplane,

93
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:28,300
I've got to reduce the radar cross-section.

94
00:08:28,820 --> 00:08:33,400
So the first thing we do is to try to make sure that all the lines and all the surfaces

95
00:08:33,401 --> 00:08:37,340
are inclined at a very shallow angle to incoming radar.

96
00:08:37,880 --> 00:08:40,480
So most of the energy is reflected off into space.

97
00:08:41,060 --> 00:08:48,940
Huge mainframe computers are used to calculate the vast range of design possibilities and

98
00:08:48,941 --> 00:08:51,740
their impact on radar visibility.

99
00:08:51,741 --> 00:08:57,820
The computer capability of our mainframes is what can be held in anybody's hand calculator

100
00:08:57,821 --> 00:08:59,700
these days.

101
00:08:59,701 --> 00:09:05,540
And the reason why the airplane was faceted the way it was was because we could only handle

102
00:09:05,541 --> 00:09:09,100
a certain number of surfaces in one computation.

103
00:09:11,060 --> 00:09:19,760
After months of numbers crunching, the Skunk Works team concludes a flat diamond is the optimum shape to evade radar.

104
00:09:22,340 --> 00:09:30,340
But Lockheed's futuristic concept has no aerodynamic characteristics and is quickly dubbed the hopeless diamond.

105
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:38,540
The shape is obviously strange by any historical standards.

106
00:09:38,541 --> 00:09:42,760
when you first look at it and i'm not an aerodynamicist but i've been around the

107
00:09:42,761 --> 00:09:48,620
aircraft business a long time i had the same reaction a lot of other people have had and that

108
00:09:48,621 --> 00:09:54,440
is is this going to fly i mean this is a really strange looking airplane

109
00:09:54,441 --> 00:10:02,400
lockheed was losing money in their commercial business the pressure was on for the skunkworks

110
00:10:02,401 --> 00:10:07,620
team they had certainly designed something revolutionary but could they possibly build

111
00:10:07,621 --> 00:10:09,640
a true stealth fighter aircraft.

112
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:19,440
Would the hopeless diamond defy its critics and fly?

113
00:10:21,720 --> 00:10:27,180
In March 1990, an SR-71 Blackbird set a coast-to-coast speed record

114
00:10:27,181 --> 00:10:31,600
when it flew from Los Angeles to Washington, D.C. in just 64 minutes.

115
00:10:31,601 --> 00:10:36,140
Its average speed was 2,144 miles per hour.

116
00:10:37,620 --> 00:10:45,120
By the mid-1970s, the Cold War was getting hot.

117
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:51,400
Soviet nuclear testing and arms proliferation is threatening international relations.

118
00:10:55,840 --> 00:10:59,420
America's fear of the red threat was definitely alive.

119
00:10:59,421 --> 00:11:07,960
In this, the skunkworks at Lockheed labors 24-7 to turn a flattened diamond outline into

120
00:11:07,961 --> 00:11:11,020
a flying delta wing stealth aircraft.

121
00:11:11,021 --> 00:11:17,760
If we take this diamond shape and chop some pieces out of it, that's pretty much how

122
00:11:17,761 --> 00:11:19,420
the airplane was evolved.

123
00:11:19,421 --> 00:11:26,460
We just started from that very simple concept and chopped it around until we got something

124
00:11:26,461 --> 00:11:29,380
that looked like an airplane.

125
00:11:29,381 --> 00:11:38,320
In April 1976, armed with these new futuristic outlines, DARPA declares Lockheed the winner of its competition.

126
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:45,960
Under the codename Have Blue, the design stage will be supervised by the Air Force.

127
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:52,880
Fearing espionage, the Air Force imposes the highest level of security imaginable.

128
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:59,340
Everything stays inside the building and the building has no windows.

129
00:11:59,380 --> 00:12:02,520
and you just don't talk about it when you're outside

130
00:12:02,521 --> 00:12:06,640
and you have the minimum amount of government people who actually interact with you.

131
00:12:08,620 --> 00:12:11,640
Very few of the senior management Lockheed Corporation

132
00:12:11,641 --> 00:12:16,380
in the corporate office or elsewhere knew of the existence of this program.

133
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:17,800
They did not know it existed.

134
00:12:19,780 --> 00:12:23,960
But the staff at Skunk Works knows all about sensitive operations.

135
00:12:25,140 --> 00:12:28,940
Well, there's always sort of a wartime environment at the Skunk Works,

136
00:12:28,941 --> 00:12:32,800
which goes back to the U-2 operations and the SR operations.

137
00:12:33,380 --> 00:12:36,400
We're always doing things in a clandestine sense,

138
00:12:36,840 --> 00:12:39,360
and we're always sort of, quote, at war

139
00:12:39,361 --> 00:12:42,620
when the rest of the services and the rest of the country is not.

140
00:12:44,580 --> 00:12:48,480
Now Have Blue is the most top-secret program in the country.

141
00:12:49,600 --> 00:12:51,200
Under extraordinary conditions,

142
00:12:51,620 --> 00:12:54,780
two technology demonstrator aircraft are to be produced.

143
00:12:55,340 --> 00:12:58,820
One, to test the basic flying qualities of the design,

144
00:12:58,940 --> 00:13:01,740
The other, it's stealth.

145
00:13:03,960 --> 00:13:06,600
But there are some major challenges ahead.

146
00:13:07,900 --> 00:13:12,960
Alan Brown tackles the crucial problem of reducing the plane's radar cross-section.

147
00:13:14,300 --> 00:13:20,300
The inlets on an airplane looking nose-on are really the dominant source of return.

148
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:27,020
The energy goes in the inlets and rattles around and just comes right back like these two lighthouse beams.

149
00:13:27,021 --> 00:13:32,020
So if you can reduce that, then you've pretty much taken care of the rest of the opening.

150
00:13:32,021 --> 00:13:39,020
So I came up with a fiberglass grid, which incorporates an absorbing material.

151
00:13:39,021 --> 00:13:47,020
And we used to call it the Roach Motel, because there was an ad at that time on TV for the Roach Motel.

152
00:13:47,021 --> 00:13:52,020
Roach Motel. The roaches check in, but they don't check out.

153
00:13:52,021 --> 00:13:54,800
And that was the theme with the radar waves.

154
00:13:55,580 --> 00:14:00,020
The waves were absorbed to a large extent by the filter at the front,

155
00:14:00,380 --> 00:14:04,200
but what did get through to the engine face could not come out again.

156
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:10,860
Finally, RAM, radar-absorbing materials, are added to minimize the remaining radar signature.

157
00:14:12,520 --> 00:14:16,040
The radar-absorbing material was made in sheets like linoleum,

158
00:14:16,560 --> 00:14:21,640
and then these sheets were cut to fit the facets of the airplane, the flat surfaces.

159
00:14:22,020 --> 00:14:24,080
and then they were put on adhesively.

160
00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:27,860
So it took quite a bit of manpower to do all this work

161
00:14:27,861 --> 00:14:30,360
because the whole airplane was covered with this stuff,

162
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:32,180
and it had to be done very precisely.

163
00:14:33,500 --> 00:14:35,680
By the summer of 1977,

164
00:14:36,380 --> 00:14:39,720
American moviegoers are in the grip of Star Wars mania.

165
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:45,260
Meanwhile, their own government is turning science fiction into science fact.

166
00:14:45,261 --> 00:14:56,880
In November, the Hav Blue prototype is secretly transported to the Nevada desert for a series of covert test flights.

167
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:11,460
The following month, as America goes about its business, unaware of the highly classified project, the world's first stealth aircraft makes its maiden flight.

168
00:15:11,461 --> 00:15:18,680
the have blue demonstrated that we could indeed take an aircraft that was

169
00:15:18,681 --> 00:15:24,180
shaped the way it needed to be shaped turned out to be a very unstable airplane

170
00:15:24,181 --> 00:15:34,220
and we could make that airplane fly and fly quite well but after just six months of test flights

171
00:15:34,221 --> 00:15:35,712
disaster strikes.

172
00:15:35,713 --> 00:15:42,120
On May 4th, 1978, the first prototype suffers engine failure and crashes.

173
00:15:42,700 --> 00:15:49,220
The pilot, Bill Park, ejects. And then two months after crashing airplane one,

174
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:54,300
after much agonizing and soul searching and reviewing and changing,

175
00:15:54,780 --> 00:15:59,660
we were airborne again in airplane two and out measuring our stealth machine.

176
00:15:59,661 --> 00:16:06,660
The plane proves almost invisible to all but the most advanced radar systems.

177
00:16:06,661 --> 00:16:10,660
And head on, HavBlue is undetectable.

178
00:16:10,661 --> 00:16:13,660
The program is a huge success.

179
00:16:17,660 --> 00:16:23,660
Then in July 1979, disaster strikes the second HavBlue prototype.

180
00:16:23,661 --> 00:16:32,640
The airplane was conducting a routine stealth flight where it flies past the radar systems on the base.

181
00:16:33,680 --> 00:16:37,780
And Ken noticed that he was losing control.

182
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:40,800
He got a fire indication back at one of the exhausts.

183
00:16:40,801 --> 00:16:50,440
I realized things were bad and I did start talking to the home plate and let them know that things weren't going well.

184
00:16:50,441 --> 00:16:55,780
and about that time the airplane went totally, totally out of control.

185
00:16:56,360 --> 00:17:01,600
And he had a very hard time even to be able to reach the control

186
00:17:01,601 --> 00:17:03,120
to be able to eject himself.

187
00:17:03,760 --> 00:17:05,020
But he finally did that.

188
00:17:06,040 --> 00:17:10,920
The airplane tumbled, much like the way Bill Park's airplane had tumbled after he ejected.

189
00:17:11,380 --> 00:17:15,860
And it was going straight down, not in a spin, just in a flopping maneuver.

190
00:17:16,920 --> 00:17:19,400
The half-blue airplane hit the ground.

191
00:17:20,440 --> 00:17:22,300
It burned pretty good.

192
00:17:23,540 --> 00:17:26,120
Both half-blue prototypes have been destroyed.

193
00:17:26,960 --> 00:17:31,340
The development of the most advanced aircraft ever built has ground to a halt.

194
00:17:31,880 --> 00:17:38,620
But from the ashes of its own destruction, Lockheed's stealth fighter is about to be reborn.

195
00:17:39,680 --> 00:17:45,040
Before it was given an official name, the F-117 was given a variety of nicknames,

196
00:17:45,041 --> 00:17:48,640
including Cockroach, Black Widow, and Wobblin' Goblin,

197
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,880
referring to the aircraft's instability at low speeds.

198
00:17:52,920 --> 00:17:59,080
In just eight months, both prototypes of the Have Blue Stealth Development Program have crashed.

199
00:17:59,720 --> 00:18:04,640
Yet despite the problems, the aircraft's invisibility to radar has been a success.

200
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:10,820
The Air Force decides to award a production contract to Lockheed.

201
00:18:10,821 --> 00:18:22,720
$340 million in covert funds are made available for the production of a full-scale development aircraft, codenamed Senior Trend.

202
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:34,380
When we first started on the F-117, or Senior Trend as it was first called, there's always a crisis.

203
00:18:34,381 --> 00:18:42,460
And so we had to have an airplane within 18 months that would penetrate certain very particular areas of the world.

204
00:18:42,980 --> 00:18:47,260
So that was one of the reasons why the senior trend was brought forward with great rapidity.

205
00:18:49,520 --> 00:18:56,580
America's growing fear of Soviet missile buildup and nuclear capability brings the Cold War to new heights of tension.

206
00:18:56,581 --> 00:19:04,080
producing a totally stealth fighter plane will give America an enormous lead in technology over

207
00:19:04,081 --> 00:19:13,320
the Soviets the stakes could not be higher Ivan was alive and well we still had the Cold War

208
00:19:13,321 --> 00:19:21,060
really big and hot and I was aware and was reminded constantly that the bad guys were

209
00:19:21,061 --> 00:19:23,820
out there looking for anything they could find.

210
00:19:25,940 --> 00:19:29,180
The project will again be black, top secret,

211
00:19:29,580 --> 00:19:32,680
and again top military brass will go to great lengths

212
00:19:32,681 --> 00:19:35,260
to prevent enemy knowledge of the covert project.

213
00:19:37,980 --> 00:19:40,860
Even the American aviation business at large

214
00:19:40,861 --> 00:19:42,720
will not know what's going on.

215
00:19:45,040 --> 00:19:47,280
Everybody had to have a secret clearance

216
00:19:47,281 --> 00:19:49,400
and everybody had to be specifically

217
00:19:49,401 --> 00:19:52,920
through the security system that allowed them into the program.

218
00:19:54,520 --> 00:19:57,800
A lot of your subcontractors don't know who they're dealing with.

219
00:19:58,100 --> 00:20:00,180
Maybe the president of the subcontractor does,

220
00:20:00,300 --> 00:20:02,280
but most of the people working on it,

221
00:20:02,460 --> 00:20:04,900
they don't know they're shipping anything to the skunkworks.

222
00:20:04,960 --> 00:20:06,180
They just know they're making something,

223
00:20:06,360 --> 00:20:07,760
and it gets put in a box and sent.

224
00:20:08,840 --> 00:20:12,460
To save development time and to avoid raising suspicions,

225
00:20:12,461 --> 00:20:16,600
the new aircraft will borrow some technology from existing jets.

226
00:20:16,601 --> 00:20:22,840
the flight control system is taken from the F-16 its cockpit features from the

227
00:20:22,841 --> 00:20:29,160
F-18 and its navigation system from the B-52

228
00:20:29,161 --> 00:20:34,380
so in general there was a philosophy that if you've got to have some radical

229
00:20:34,381 --> 00:20:38,920
stuff done which takes a lot of effort you make the rest of the airplane

230
00:20:38,921 --> 00:20:43,240
absolutely as conservative as possible because you do not want to stub your toe

231
00:20:43,241 --> 00:20:45,480
in an area which you didn't need to.

232
00:20:49,380 --> 00:20:52,340
Finally, on June 18, 1981,

233
00:20:53,060 --> 00:20:55,000
two years after launching the program,

234
00:20:55,400 --> 00:20:59,840
the Senior Trend aircraft is prepared for its first flight over the Nevada desert.

235
00:21:05,520 --> 00:21:07,560
The test is a success.

236
00:21:08,140 --> 00:21:11,160
Senior Trend has a staggeringly low radar visibility.

237
00:21:13,240 --> 00:21:15,640
Turkey, T-5B, E-7-3111.

238
00:21:17,020 --> 00:21:18,880
G-100, E-7-3111.

239
00:21:20,620 --> 00:21:25,980
The world's first stealth bomber is delivered to the Air Force in August 1982.

240
00:21:28,780 --> 00:21:33,860
And an entirely new top-secret base is built in the Nevada desert at Tonopah.

241
00:21:36,760 --> 00:21:42,820
The Tonopah test range facility was very secure in that we didn't want our adversaries to find out

242
00:21:42,821 --> 00:21:44,480
that we had stealth technology.

243
00:21:45,520 --> 00:21:48,300
We had secure procedures to enter the area,

244
00:21:48,301 --> 00:21:51,180
such as hand readers, which I had never seen before

245
00:21:51,181 --> 00:21:54,460
except on TV, and mostly in James Bond movies,

246
00:21:54,461 --> 00:21:57,920
but those procedures allowed us to enter the facility

247
00:21:57,921 --> 00:21:59,940
and keep unwanted personnel out.

248
00:22:02,440 --> 00:22:07,240
As a cover for the 117, the Air Force put in a squadron

249
00:22:07,241 --> 00:22:12,120
of A-7 attack airplanes that were supposedly testing

250
00:22:12,121 --> 00:22:15,260
some new weapons systems on these A7s.

251
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:21,420
We had a cover story in that all the pilots and maintainers

252
00:22:21,421 --> 00:22:26,160
flew the A7 aircraft, and that was what we used to fly up to Tonopah

253
00:22:26,161 --> 00:22:27,240
and to come home.

254
00:22:30,620 --> 00:22:33,660
During the day, we kept all the F-117 hangared

255
00:22:33,661 --> 00:22:36,640
so that they could not be observed by flying aircraft,

256
00:22:36,940 --> 00:22:38,640
satellites, or any other technology.

257
00:22:39,260 --> 00:22:41,880
At night, under the secrecy of darkness,

258
00:22:42,120 --> 00:22:44,320
we would bring the aircraft out and fly them,

259
00:22:44,800 --> 00:22:46,900
and we would have them hangered again before sunlight.

260
00:22:50,360 --> 00:22:55,120
I might add that the 117s weren't flown at night only for security reasons.

261
00:22:55,740 --> 00:23:01,400
There was another even compelling reason that gets lost in the woodwork.

262
00:23:02,820 --> 00:23:07,080
I remember being in my office on the F-117,

263
00:23:08,220 --> 00:23:11,300
and Bob Dixon, who was the four-star head of tactical,

264
00:23:11,301 --> 00:23:14,900
Air Force, who was talking about the color scheme.

265
00:23:15,460 --> 00:23:20,000
And he said, this airplane is going to fly at night, Brown, isn't it?

266
00:23:20,040 --> 00:23:21,460
I said, yes, sir, just fly at night.

267
00:23:21,780 --> 00:23:23,720
He says, well, paint the damn thing black.

268
00:23:24,240 --> 00:23:26,420
So he painted the damn thing black.

269
00:23:28,120 --> 00:23:33,500
First impression you get of the 117 is a Star Wars thing, the Darth Vader look about it.

270
00:23:34,140 --> 00:23:40,720
You see it head on and you immediately notice this large black, totally flat-plated,

271
00:23:40,721 --> 00:23:43,220
no rounded surfaces.

272
00:23:45,220 --> 00:23:49,500
What's unique about the F-117 is just the feeling you get

273
00:23:49,501 --> 00:23:50,880
when you walk up to it.

274
00:23:51,280 --> 00:23:52,600
Their heart really starts pounding.

275
00:23:52,920 --> 00:23:55,140
It's almost like you're approaching a woman

276
00:23:55,141 --> 00:23:56,940
that's maybe better looking than you ever thought

277
00:23:56,941 --> 00:23:58,640
you'd even be able to talk to.

278
00:23:59,100 --> 00:24:00,320
And so when you approach this woman,

279
00:24:00,400 --> 00:24:02,300
you do it carefully, but with a lot of respect.

280
00:24:03,880 --> 00:24:07,180
I always refer to it as a woman's evening dress,

281
00:24:07,181 --> 00:24:08,640
a little black dress.

282
00:24:08,641 --> 00:24:10,980
It's extremely sexy.

283
00:24:11,860 --> 00:24:13,660
It is a killing machine, though.

284
00:24:14,100 --> 00:24:16,740
Make no doubt about it, it is a killing machine.

285
00:24:16,920 --> 00:24:18,540
But it's a good-looking killing machine.

286
00:24:19,420 --> 00:24:25,100
A new unit, the 4450th Tactical Group, is created to fly the new black jet.

287
00:24:25,420 --> 00:24:26,700
And a new name is needed.

288
00:24:27,720 --> 00:24:31,860
The aircraft is given a surprising designation, the F-117.

289
00:24:35,340 --> 00:24:37,800
This was definitely an attack airplane.

290
00:24:37,801 --> 00:24:42,840
And of course the question then is why is it called an F-117 when F stands for fighter?

291
00:24:43,380 --> 00:24:47,040
And again we've got to go back to Bob Dixon who was head of TAC.

292
00:24:48,260 --> 00:24:56,200
And he was going to use his very best fighter pilots on this program because it represented the top point of the Air Force.

293
00:24:57,480 --> 00:25:02,620
Well no fighter pilot worth his salt is going to fly an airplane with a B designation in front of it.

294
00:25:02,880 --> 00:25:06,120
And maybe not an A designation which means a TAC.

295
00:25:06,121 --> 00:25:11,800
So they call it an F, to make the fighter pilots feel like they're flying fighters.

296
00:25:15,020 --> 00:25:18,180
It's a fairly roomy cockpit for a single-seat fighter.

297
00:25:18,900 --> 00:25:23,660
I thought it would be a small kind of a cockpit, say like the SR-71 or the U-2.

298
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:29,360
And when I actually got in it, there was plenty of room for all the equipment that's required to operate the aircraft.

299
00:25:30,480 --> 00:25:35,920
Without this flight control technology, the pilots would have a tough time flying the jet.

300
00:25:36,120 --> 00:25:41,060
I thought that I was going to feel a little bit disconnected, more like somebody operating

301
00:25:41,061 --> 00:25:46,400
a computer where I might have been kind of along for the ride inputting my vote here and

302
00:25:46,401 --> 00:25:47,101
there.

303
00:25:49,160 --> 00:25:52,340
I call it a wet bag of cement or a Coke machine.

304
00:25:52,341 --> 00:26:01,660
If it loses its ability to fly by wire, it is an inherently unstable platform.

305
00:26:02,040 --> 00:26:03,220
It has no glide.

306
00:26:03,221 --> 00:26:07,980
it will fall to the earth, probably not in a polite fashion, I might add.

307
00:26:09,120 --> 00:26:15,100
Nonetheless, when you're actually sitting in the cockpit and you're advancing the throttle

308
00:26:15,101 --> 00:26:19,680
or you're engaging a left turn or a right turn with the center stick,

309
00:26:20,100 --> 00:26:25,900
the aircraft flies just like any other fighter that I have flown in the past.

310
00:26:25,901 --> 00:26:37,641
The aircraft is so well designed and modeled that you really feel like you're strapped to the machine and you are putting the aircraft and the weapons exactly where

311
00:26:37,642 --> 00:26:38,500
you want to.

312
00:26:43,500 --> 00:26:47,940
Nighthawks are armed with a weapons system that lets them strike at the heart of the enemy.

313
00:26:47,941 --> 00:27:00,140
High-tech laser-assisted mapping and guidance systems allow the black jets to release deep penetration bunker busters with pinpoint accuracy.

314
00:27:02,960 --> 00:27:09,520
The early flights of the F-117 prove that its principal function blows the competition away.

315
00:27:10,160 --> 00:27:12,640
The aircraft is all but invisible to radar.

316
00:27:13,640 --> 00:27:17,440
Stealth's development has, by any standards, been lightning fast.

317
00:27:17,940 --> 00:27:23,060
But the program has also been expensive.

318
00:27:23,061 --> 00:27:28,180
To date, the total cost has been over $6.5 billion.

319
00:27:28,181 --> 00:27:33,020
With additional aircraft ordered, this will soon rise.

320
00:27:33,021 --> 00:27:41,320
By the late 1980s, America has its conceptual aircraft.

321
00:27:41,321 --> 00:27:46,340
But one question remains.

322
00:27:46,341 --> 00:27:54,040
How will the most expensive aircraft in military history perform in combat?

323
00:27:54,041 --> 00:27:59,460
The answer would come sooner than anyone could imagine.

324
00:27:59,461 --> 00:28:05,100
Since radar emissions would give away its location, the F-117 does not carry radar.

325
00:28:05,101 --> 00:28:13,140
Instead, the aircraft navigates primarily by GPS and high-accuracy inertial navigation.

326
00:28:13,141 --> 00:28:20,460
November 10th, 1988, the Air Force announces the existence of its top secret F-117 at a

327
00:28:20,461 --> 00:28:25,460
Pentagon press conference, much to the surprise of many.

328
00:28:25,461 --> 00:28:39,461
I was on vacation in South America at the time and first saw it in a Portuguese language

329
00:28:39,760 --> 00:28:42,461
newspaper, a photograph of our airplane.

330
00:28:42,462 --> 00:28:46,040
And my immediate reaction is to try and call somebody

331
00:28:46,041 --> 00:28:50,360
in Washington and say, do you know there's a picture of our airplane in a Brazilian newspaper?

332
00:28:51,320 --> 00:28:54,947
A total of 59 aircraft are being procured.

333
00:28:54,948 --> 00:28:58,920
52 of those have been delivered and seven more

334
00:28:58,921 --> 00:29:00,259
are in production.

335
00:29:00,260 --> 00:29:05,540
The reason, of course, was that as the Air Force expanded the number of

336
00:29:05,541 --> 00:29:09,480
airplanes and pilots, they decide they better start flying during the day.

337
00:29:11,300 --> 00:29:17,600
By the fall of 1989, America's stealth fighter is no longer shrouded in secrecy.

338
00:29:18,220 --> 00:29:22,660
The combat-ready F-117 is about to be unleashed.

339
00:29:23,180 --> 00:29:31,040
As president, I have no higher obligation than to safeguard the lives of American citizens.

340
00:29:31,041 --> 00:29:37,340
and that is why I directed our armed forces to protect the lives of American citizens in Panama

341
00:29:37,341 --> 00:29:41,640
and to bring General Noriega to justice in the United States.

342
00:29:44,080 --> 00:29:51,700
Though Panama has no radar-guided defense network, F-117s would spearhead Operation Just Cause.

343
00:29:52,500 --> 00:29:56,040
The aircraft was chosen not because it was stealthy,

344
00:29:56,041 --> 00:30:01,340
but because it could drop precision munitions at a dedicated time over target.

345
00:30:01,960 --> 00:30:03,800
Those two were keys to the mission.

346
00:30:04,300 --> 00:30:06,760
We had to make sure the weapons went where we aimed them,

347
00:30:07,100 --> 00:30:11,240
and we had to be on time because 30 seconds after our bombs went off,

348
00:30:11,640 --> 00:30:16,060
we had Navy SEALs and Delta Forces jumping out of aircraft into those fields.

349
00:30:18,300 --> 00:30:22,040
Two black jets are ordered to destroy the barracks at Rio Hato,

350
00:30:22,041 --> 00:30:25,840
belonging to Battalion 2000, a unit loyal to Noriega.

351
00:30:26,040 --> 00:30:36,576
Just prior to taking off of that mission, we were told by the leadership that they wanted to maintain the barracks and keep them to use in the future for the American

352
00:30:36,577 --> 00:30:37,277
forces.

353
00:30:37,520 --> 00:30:46,520
Our goal was to stun and disorient the Panamanian Defense Forces, so they asked us to drop our bombs in the fields 50 meters short of these barracks.

354
00:30:47,680 --> 00:30:52,160
Two 2,000-pound bombs are dropped, and the field is hit.

355
00:30:52,161 --> 00:30:59,560
But back home, the F-117's first operational mission is considered a failure.

356
00:31:00,940 --> 00:31:05,080
Bombing fields is not what expensive stealth bombers are supposed to do.

357
00:31:06,520 --> 00:31:14,300
After Operation Just Cause, people and the media tried to say that the stealth fighter was not successful.

358
00:31:15,160 --> 00:31:19,780
The media was looking for a story on wasting taxpayers' money and buying this technology.

359
00:31:22,160 --> 00:31:26,280
The stealth program is criticized by Congress and labeled too expensive.

360
00:31:28,020 --> 00:31:30,640
The total program cost about $8 billion.

361
00:31:31,040 --> 00:31:34,620
When I say total cost, I mean the total development cost, total flight test,

362
00:31:34,740 --> 00:31:37,040
produced 59 airplanes, all the spares.

363
00:31:37,760 --> 00:31:41,860
That even includes $200 million to build the hangars at Tonopah.

364
00:31:42,600 --> 00:31:44,800
And it is a true bottom line number.

365
00:31:45,700 --> 00:31:48,440
I know this was in the 1980s and so on,

366
00:31:48,441 --> 00:31:55,960
But even so, if you compare that with other aircraft programs, most aircraft development programs cost today $20 billion.

367
00:31:57,800 --> 00:32:01,520
But the Air Force successfully champions the Nighthawk's case.

368
00:32:02,120 --> 00:32:05,660
The fact is, the F-117 is accurate and cheap.

369
00:32:10,040 --> 00:32:15,840
On August 2nd, 1990, Saddam Hussein occupies oil-rich Kuwait.

370
00:32:18,440 --> 00:32:28,860
Just a month later, the 37th Tactical Wing begins a very public deployment to the King Khalid military airbase in Saudi Arabia.

371
00:32:33,420 --> 00:32:38,580
The disappointments of Panama will not be repeated during Operation Desert Storm.

372
00:32:40,420 --> 00:32:44,860
It's at the tip of the fighting spear in terms of knocking the door down.

373
00:32:44,861 --> 00:32:49,400
If you've got to go downtown, you've got to take out those strategic targets,

374
00:32:49,920 --> 00:32:53,960
and you've got to penetrate that integrated air defense system,

375
00:32:54,240 --> 00:32:57,840
the F-117 is the weapon system of choice.

376
00:33:01,900 --> 00:33:08,180
It is our ability to reach out and touch the bad guy before he even knows worth it.

377
00:33:08,540 --> 00:33:10,040
It's kind of like being the thief in the night.

378
00:33:10,041 --> 00:33:13,980
You're stealing that guy's capability to shoot back at you.

379
00:33:14,860 --> 00:33:24,780
22 minutes after midnight on January 17th 1991 F-117s from the 415 TFS attack.

380
00:33:27,700 --> 00:33:31,960
All of their targets are hit with an unprecedented degree of precision.

381
00:33:36,060 --> 00:33:41,140
We can put that bomb right on that very spot that we're intending to,

382
00:33:41,560 --> 00:33:43,840
be it through the window, on the air conditioner, on the top of the building,

383
00:33:43,841 --> 00:33:48,920
and that we can destroy or take out the precise objective.

384
00:33:51,080 --> 00:33:53,120
And we can do this right downtown.

385
00:33:56,500 --> 00:33:58,620
During the war to liberate Kuwait,

386
00:33:58,621 --> 00:34:04,920
a total of 45 F-117s fly 1,271 combat sorties,

387
00:34:05,520 --> 00:34:10,720
totaling over 6,900 flying hours and dropping 2,000 tons of bombs.

388
00:34:13,840 --> 00:34:19,600
representing just two percent of the attacking force

389
00:34:19,601 --> 00:34:22,560
night hawks dropped almost forty percent

390
00:34:22,561 --> 00:34:29,560
of the total number of bombs used in the conflict

391
00:34:31,840 --> 00:34:36,600
throughout the nineteen nineties f-117s are found wherever the US Air Force is

392
00:34:36,601 --> 00:34:41,340
in action

393
00:34:41,341 --> 00:34:48,080
But on the night of March 27, 1999, during the NATO bombing of Serbia, the unthinkable happens.

394
00:34:49,180 --> 00:34:53,800
Have the Serbs developed a new radar system capable of detecting the undetectable?

395
00:34:55,260 --> 00:34:59,360
Has radar caught up with America's stealth aircraft?

396
00:34:59,361 --> 00:35:13,247
During Operation Desert Storm in 1991, F-117s flew 18 and a half hours non-stop from Holloman Air Force Base in New Mexico to Kuwait, setting a record for single-seat

397
00:35:13,248 --> 00:35:14,000
fighters.

398
00:35:16,920 --> 00:35:19,560
Kosovo, March 28, 1999.

399
00:35:21,120 --> 00:35:27,980
25 miles west of Belgrade in the village of Bouginovitz, a downed F-117 lies in pieces.

400
00:35:29,360 --> 00:35:39,220
Serbian television transmits pictures of the wreckage around the world.

401
00:35:39,221 --> 00:35:44,100
The images send shockwaves throughout the US as the Pentagon refuses to explain the

402
00:35:44,101 --> 00:35:45,440
cause of the crash.

403
00:35:45,441 --> 00:35:58,240
During the course of the 1990s, there had been six major incidents involving F-117s.

404
00:35:58,241 --> 00:36:04,460
At an air show in Baltimore in September 1997, a black jet disintegrated in midair, crashing

405
00:36:04,461 --> 00:36:06,840
in a residential area.

406
00:36:06,841 --> 00:36:17,920
Could the Dowd jet in Kosovo have been the result of a similar catastrophic failure?

407
00:36:17,921 --> 00:36:21,680
The information surrounding that, of course, is still classified.

408
00:36:21,681 --> 00:36:24,360
A bullet doesn't have a radar, and it doesn't care where you're at.

409
00:36:24,361 --> 00:36:27,300
And the chances of one of those hitting me ballistically are just out of the blue.

410
00:36:27,620 --> 00:36:28,360
It's pretty small.

411
00:36:30,360 --> 00:36:33,480
But he got hit, so it can happen.

412
00:36:37,600 --> 00:36:40,640
Obviously, the loss of an F-117 is going to impact the community.

413
00:36:41,120 --> 00:36:46,280
We don't own a lot of them as a country or as an Air Force, so losing one is significant.

414
00:36:47,620 --> 00:36:54,120
Whatever the cause of the crash, it did not affect the operational status of the world's first stealth aircraft.

415
00:36:54,360 --> 00:37:01,640
Post-September 11th, Saddam Hussein is once again seen as a threat in the Middle East.

416
00:37:02,540 --> 00:37:07,720
As fears about his weapons of mass destruction grow, America's patience runs out.

417
00:37:09,720 --> 00:37:14,000
Coalition forces have begun striking selective targets of military importance

418
00:37:14,001 --> 00:37:17,480
to undermine Saddam Hussein's ability to wage war.

419
00:37:17,481 --> 00:37:25,280
Now that conflict has come, the only way to limit its duration is to apply decisive force.

420
00:37:26,360 --> 00:37:31,280
In Operation Iraqi Freedom, F-117s would draw first blood.

421
00:37:33,620 --> 00:37:36,700
Flexibility is what the F-117 gave us in Iraqi Freedom.

422
00:37:37,360 --> 00:37:43,400
The very opening night of the war, where they had a key target that they needed taken out,

423
00:37:43,401 --> 00:37:48,440
They had to figure out very quickly what kind of airplane was going to go in there and take out that target.

424
00:37:48,860 --> 00:37:50,420
And their choice was the F-117.

425
00:37:52,620 --> 00:37:56,780
Based on intelligence, a gathering of Iraqi leadership is identified.

426
00:37:57,740 --> 00:38:04,140
In a military decapitation exercise, stealth will be used to try to eliminate the key players of the regime.

427
00:38:05,680 --> 00:38:12,980
I mean, man, if you could hit the one target that completely ceased all hostilities, that'd be the juiciest target of all.

428
00:38:13,400 --> 00:38:17,300
If I could go in there one night and stop the war, that would be money.

429
00:38:18,300 --> 00:38:22,180
If it was the one right downtown, right in the center, the hardest one to get to,

430
00:38:22,940 --> 00:38:26,020
that's the job of the F-117, is to hit that target.

431
00:38:26,920 --> 00:38:28,660
That was the objective of night one.

432
00:38:29,380 --> 00:38:33,400
If we can cut the head off the snake, the game's over.

433
00:38:33,401 --> 00:38:35,808
At precisely 4 a.m.

434
00:38:35,809 --> 00:38:44,680
local time, four 2,000-pound bunker busters hit an underground complex

435
00:38:44,681 --> 00:38:47,960
thought to contain Saddam Hussein and his top generals.

436
00:38:52,220 --> 00:38:56,060
Though the mission was a success, its objective failed.

437
00:38:56,800 --> 00:39:00,680
A defiant Saddam appeared on television a few hours after the strike.

438
00:39:00,681 --> 00:39:04,400
Iraq's dictator was still very much alive.

439
00:39:10,440 --> 00:39:13,180
Twelve years after the first Gulf War,

440
00:39:13,700 --> 00:39:18,020
new technologies would be employed to utterly destroy Iraq's ruling elite.

441
00:39:21,260 --> 00:39:25,460
But Baghdad, one of the most heavily defended cities in the world,

442
00:39:25,461 --> 00:39:27,200
is no easy target.

443
00:39:30,680 --> 00:39:36,620
The F-117 is a very mission planning intensive platform.

444
00:39:36,621 --> 00:39:45,220
Our tactics are all talked about, contrived, built on the ground and put into the computer.

445
00:39:45,221 --> 00:39:49,780
We put our tactics in this little black box, stick it in the aircraft, it sends it to the

446
00:39:49,781 --> 00:39:56,200
computer, I take off, I turn the autopilot on and it's basically like pushing the go

447
00:39:56,201 --> 00:39:58,560
button on the jet.

448
00:39:58,561 --> 00:40:04,580
When you're flying it, the autopilot is flying the airplane doing exactly what the mission

449
00:40:04,581 --> 00:40:05,620
planners told it to do.

450
00:40:06,240 --> 00:40:09,700
However, what it isn't doing is not guiding the bombs to their target.

451
00:40:10,280 --> 00:40:14,520
So you have a very short period of time where you are doing your job.

452
00:40:15,400 --> 00:40:18,120
The rest of it is letting the airplane fly.

453
00:40:21,280 --> 00:40:28,200
Going downtown the first night for me, I had a lot of anxiety because I wasn't,

454
00:40:28,201 --> 00:40:31,100
but I didn't have complete faith in the technology.

455
00:40:32,460 --> 00:40:33,780
One had been shot down before.

456
00:40:34,640 --> 00:40:36,640
So I was a little nervous about going downtown,

457
00:40:36,800 --> 00:40:37,760
because you just don't know what to expect.

458
00:40:38,060 --> 00:40:40,000
And all the while, you're flying directly toward the city

459
00:40:40,001 --> 00:40:41,060
for all the bad things happening.

460
00:40:42,540 --> 00:40:46,220
And we knew Saddam didn't like our black jet.

461
00:40:46,960 --> 00:40:48,680
So here I am, knowing that, I mean,

462
00:40:48,960 --> 00:40:50,540
well, wouldn't I look good with an apple in my mouth

463
00:40:50,541 --> 00:40:52,880
on a silver platter on his table, you know what I mean?

464
00:40:55,180 --> 00:40:56,660
So looking out over the distance

465
00:40:56,661 --> 00:40:58,780
and seeing this circular city of lights,

466
00:40:59,280 --> 00:41:00,700
and I think it might have been the oil fires,

467
00:41:01,140 --> 00:41:02,500
there was a glow around the city.

468
00:41:02,880 --> 00:41:04,500
That just added to this realism.

469
00:41:07,000 --> 00:41:10,960
You hit a predetermined point that we call an IP,

470
00:41:11,180 --> 00:41:12,560
which stands for initial point.

471
00:41:13,060 --> 00:41:14,560
Once you cross that point,

472
00:41:14,660 --> 00:41:17,620
the aircraft will make an appropriate turn or descent

473
00:41:17,621 --> 00:41:20,940
to align itself on its attack axis.

474
00:41:20,941 --> 00:41:22,740
All does it automatically.

475
00:41:23,200 --> 00:41:25,460
You're just systems managing at that point.

476
00:41:26,660 --> 00:41:33,660
And the next thing you know is, AAA starts lighting up over the town.

477
00:41:33,661 --> 00:41:37,660
And I thought to myself, wow, that's a lot of lead in here.

478
00:41:37,661 --> 00:41:42,660
And you keep looking at that point that turns you left into Baghdad.

479
00:41:42,661 --> 00:41:46,660
And you just stare at that thing going, boy, here it comes, here it comes.

480
00:41:46,661 --> 00:41:50,660
The next thing you know, you look to your right, lots of AAA, lots of SAMs,

481
00:41:50,661 --> 00:41:52,660
and the jet turns right into it.

482
00:41:52,661 --> 00:41:54,660
And you're like, oh, my God, down the chute we go.

483
00:41:54,661 --> 00:42:01,160
Once it makes that initial turn onto its attack axis, you run through a series of steps.

484
00:42:01,280 --> 00:42:08,940
You want to check, for example, your fuel, your altitude, your time, where you're at in the timeline, and your speed.

485
00:42:09,740 --> 00:42:16,500
That will run you through this ballet of sorts to get you into that bombing run mode.

486
00:42:16,501 --> 00:42:24,600
I'm so thankful that I'm on the delivering end when I'm flying the F-117

487
00:42:24,601 --> 00:42:28,220
because I would not want to be asleep in the middle of the night

488
00:42:28,221 --> 00:42:29,660
or awake for that matter

489
00:42:29,661 --> 00:42:33,280
and then have something that I never heard coming, never saw it

490
00:42:33,281 --> 00:42:37,700
although in another respect maybe that's the quick and painless way to go if you're the enemy

491
00:42:37,701 --> 00:42:43,880
No official statistics on the effectiveness of Nighthawks during Iraqi freedom have been released

492
00:42:43,881 --> 00:42:46,360
but its contribution was immense.

493
00:42:47,260 --> 00:42:51,720
No other aircraft in the U.S. arsenal can do the job it does so well.

494
00:42:54,100 --> 00:43:00,660
The F-117 was the first aircraft that was just purely designed around the idea of stealth

495
00:43:00,661 --> 00:43:06,960
and being able to go into a highly defended target area with near invisible capability,

496
00:43:07,460 --> 00:43:10,080
deliver precision weapons, and then get back out safely.

497
00:43:10,740 --> 00:43:13,720
And obviously it's proven itself several times in that scenario.

498
00:43:13,880 --> 00:43:21,600
The aircraft designed in absolute secrecy at the height of the Cold War is as potent

499
00:43:21,601 --> 00:43:25,380
a weapon today as it has ever been.

500
00:43:25,381 --> 00:43:31,180
The F-117 is capable of utterly destroying defense infrastructures without ever being

501
00:43:31,181 --> 00:43:32,180
detected.

502
00:43:32,181 --> 00:43:36,820
For its pilots, the advantage in modern warfare is unbeatable.

